## Fix round 1 — pre-parse graph authentication and Git environment isolation

Focused source commit: `9b99c12324f41237ac0b4e42fe756f6ab8999372`

### Review findings addressed

1. Build now performs an explicit complete dependency-authentication phase before its first typed
   load. It authenticates the execution, evaluator, baseline, panel-plan, and sandbox root bytes;
   reads only their already-authenticated canonical primitives to route references; authenticates
   the PIT bundle, prices provenance, and every mechanics/quick/discovery panel as typed raw edges;
   and authenticates the baseline source bundle and any existing baseline policy descriptor. The
   evaluator/execution/sandbox leaves must not contain undeclared child references, and the panel
   and baseline child sets must exactly equal the authenticated primitive's named references.

   The only permitted absent edge is the exact `policy_revision_ref` named by the authenticated
   baseline authority, because `build-manifest` owns that create-only descriptor write. An absent
   baseline source, panel/data edge, or any relocated/digest-mismatched child fails before a typed
   authority or panel is constructed and before any output write. After typed construction, the
   baseline's decoded policy reference must equal the preauthenticated create-only edge; an
   existing descriptor is decoded and must equal the inline baseline policy before composition.
   The policy-scope artifact remains a build output and is created only after this input graph and
   all cross-artifact bindings succeed.

2. Clean-source capture no longer passes inherited Git settings. It removes every ambient variable
   whose name begins with `GIT_`, then installs a closed environment that disables system/global
   config and attributes, replace-object rewriting, and optional locks. This covers repository and
   worktree selectors including `GIT_DIR`, `GIT_WORK_TREE`, `GIT_COMMON_DIR`, `GIT_CONFIG`,
   `GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS`, ceiling/discovery overrides, index/object/alternate-object paths, and
   executable-path overrides.

   Every Git subprocess uses the explicit absolute executable, `-C` with the explicit source root,
   an explicit `cwd`, `shell=False`, and the sanitized environment. Before HEAD, status, or source
   blob reads, the implementation requires the supplied source root to be its canonical resolved
   path, requires Git's absolute `--show-toplevel` result to resolve exactly to that root, and
   requires `--is-inside-work-tree` to return true. A redirected worktree therefore fails before
   source bytes are accepted.

### Direct synthetic evidence

A bounded disposable non-test script used authenticated in-memory root primitives and raw edges,
then replaced the baseline source child with independent missing and digest-mismatch failures. Its
typed loader and all write/panel-parse methods were assertion bombs. Both failures propagated with
the typed-load counter still zero.

The same script mocked the Git process boundary, populated a hostile environment with repository,
worktree, common-dir, config, index, object, alternate-object, ceiling, discovery, and executable
overrides, and asserted that none reached the subprocess. It completed a source snapshot using only
mocked exact-root responses, then independently returned a different existing top-level and proved
the capture failed before HEAD/status/show.

Observed output:

`synthetic-task2-fix1: transitive-missing-mismatch=pre-parse-rejected typed-loads=0 git-env=sanitized resolved-worktree=exact hostile-redirection=rejected`

No real Git command, materialization, provider, network, Docker, data evaluation, replay, or test
file was used by this check. The disposable script was removed after the final run.

